      {
        "id": "hidden1",
        "operation": "validated_unit",
        "summary": "The failure originates in `cap_reached` (ledger.py), which uses `spent > cap`. The requirement counts reaching the cap exactly as reached, so the operator must be `>=`: `return spent >= cap`. With that change, `issue(ledger, cap=1.0)` at `spent = 1.0` raises `PermitError(\"cumulative_cap_exceeded\")` and `test_refuses_at_exact_cap` passes. `issue` itself needs no change.",
        "source_ids": [
          "request",
          "requirement",
          "code",
          "test"
        ],
        "data": {
          "summary": "`cap_reached` in ledger.py uses `spent > cap`, which treats exact equality as not-reached; the requirement says reaching the cap exactly counts as reached, so test_refuses_at_exact_cap (spent=1.0, cap=1.0) sees no PermitError. Corrected operator: `>=` \u2014 i.e. `return spent >= cap`.",
          "claims": [
            "Faulty function: cap_reached(spent, cap) in ledger.py; issue() is correct and only propagates the wrong predicate.",
            "Corrected comparison operator is >= (return spent >= cap).",
            "Assumes float comparison is acceptable as written; no tolerance/rounding handling was specified in the requirement."
          ],
          "unresolved": [],
          "parent_hashes": [
            "27d47c7dc809b42b28a38140e8c6f670108b528dbabab34e734aadfcdd3312f7"
          ],
